      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hello Michael!\n\nI’d love to see the perf data, but that link didn\u0027t allowed me to capture anything meaningful. Could I schedule a quick sync so you can walk me through it?\n\nEnabling PA in Skia benchmarks gets us closer to how it actually runs in Chrome (assuming Chrome is an optimization target here).  Previously, V8 asked for something equivalent for their benchmark, even if we won\u0027t rewrite their pointers.\n\nTo escalate regressions to PA maintainers, you can reach out to `chrome-memory-safety@` (126 member) or `chrome-cpp-runtime-safety@` (16 members). Alternatively, just email `arthursonzogni@` or `nusko@` directly and we’ll loop in the right folks.\n\nRegarding Blink: the info you saw might be from outdated docs? Blink is already rewritten and enforced. GC memory pointers don\u0027t use `raw_ptr` as it would be useless, but the rest do. `third_party/blink` has ~600 `raw_ptr` instances and 26 exclusions. We also disabled specific directories for perf: `third_party/blink/renderer/{core,platform/{heap,wtf,fonts}}`.\n\nFully agree on proceeding carefully. We should aim for zero regressions and not being worried to revert quickly if we hit any. Every `raw_ptr` can be opted out, so nothing is irreversible. \n\nIn Chrome, before landing patches, I find hotspot by diffing profiling data locally, Pinpoint to find additional benchmark / platforms with differences. After landing the patches, I check Stack Sampled Metrics to find what minor hotspot appears on real users.\nI heard Skia doesn\u0027t have a Pinpoint equivalent. How do you usually track these down reliably in general? Happy to learn more about your workflow.",
      "parentUuid": "638e408a_0bd4020c",
      "revId": "950eb6142ccd0142dc9fe9b269694df4b626f50d",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
